<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">
<title>{{ t("library.page.library-targum", "Library — targum") }}</title>
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('tokens.css') }}{{ asset('shared.css') }}{{ asset('chrome.css') }}{{ asset('words.css') }}{{ asset('library.css') }}</style>
{{ hebrew_face() }}
<script>{{ asset('keep.js') }}</script>
{#- The key, before anything that reads it (targum-internal#343). `building.js`, and
    the conversation's pill, the followed series and the palette in the nav, each read `window.TARGUM_KEY`
    once as they load; it was set in a script at the foot of the body, so on a local serve
    they held an empty key for the life of the page and the bell, the followed series and
    the chat list were refused. The hosted box has no key and was never affected. -#}
<script>window.TARGUM_KEY = "{{ token }}";</script>
</head>
<body class="shelf">
{% set here = 'library' %}{% set title = t("nav.library", "Library") %}
{% include '_nav.html.j2' %}
<script>{{ asset('building.js') }}</script>

<main>
  {#- One list, not two. What the reader has built and what is waiting to be built are
      the same question — what shall I read — and splitting them into a shelf and a
      catalogue meant answering it twice, in two different shapes. -#}
  {#- The catalogue, which is everybody's. The reader's own texts were a second tab here,
      Your uploads, until 2026-09-25, and are on Your targums now (design.md §12, "Yours
      and everyone's"). The tab strip stays for the Beit Midrash, and is hidden where it
      would hold one tab. -#}
  <div class="tabs" id="where" role="tablist" aria-label="{{ t("library.page.which-texts", "Which texts") }}"></div>

  <section class="panel" id="picked">
    {#- The way back from a See all list to the shelves. -#}
    <p class="see-back" id="see-back" hidden><a href="/library" id="see-back-link"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> {{ t("nav.library", "Library") }}</a></p>

    <p class="note" id="picked-note">{{ t("library.page.tap-a-text-to-read-it", "Tap a text to open it.") }}</p>

    {#- How many texts there are, and how much of them is being shown — with the second
        half of that sentence as the control (design.md §12, 2026-09-17). "I want to be
        able to immediately choose a reading AT MY LEVEL" is answered by the list opening
        already narrowed and saying so, rather than by a filter that could be found. -#}
    <p class="said" id="said"></p>
    {#- Where you are in the Beit Midrash, once inside a door (targum-internal#340). -#}
    <nav class="crumbs" id="crumbs" aria-label="{{ t("library.page.where-you-are", "Where you are") }}" hidden></nav>

    {#- What the texts are about. The row a reader browses by, above everything that
        narrows: drawn from the subjects that have rows behind them, never from the
        catalogue's whole vocabulary, and each one carrying its count. -#}
    <p class="chips-label" id="subject-label">{{ t("library.page.what-its-about", "What it's about") }}</p>
    <div class="chips subjects" id="subject-chips" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("library.page.what-its-about", "What it's about") }}"></div>

    {#- The search box is the one search, opened already held to the Library (design.md
        §12, "One search, everywhere", 2026-10-09): pressing it opens the search over the
        page, where it narrowed this list in place until that day. -#}
    <div class="sift">
      <input
        id="find" class="well find" type="search" dir="auto" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
        placeholder="{{ t("library.page.search-box", "Search the Library") }}" aria-label="{{ t("library.page.search-box", "Search the Library") }}" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-controls="palette">
      {#- The Hebrew of the Mishnah, of Maimonides, of Mendele and of this morning's paper
          are not four genres, they are close to four languages, and which one somebody is
          learning is the first question this page asks. A switch with its own label rather
          than a third row of pills: as pills it sat beside the kind filter with a second
          chip also saying "All", and the two read as one row of ten. -#}
      {#- The filters, folded behind one word (2026-09-11): the search is the page; the
          rest opens when asked. A `details`, so it needs no script and keeps its state
          on the page. -#}
      <details class="filters" id="filters">
        <summary aria-label="{{ t("library.page.filters", "Filters") }}"
                 data-more="{{ t("library.page.filters", "Filters") }}"
                 data-fewer="{{ t("library.page.fewer-filters", "Fewer filters") }}"></summary>
      <div class="switch-set">
        <span class="switch-label">{{ t("library.page.which-hebrew", "Which Hebrew") }}</span>
        <div class="seg" id="register-chips" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("library.page.which-hebrew", "Which Hebrew") }}"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chips" id="kind-chips" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("library.page.what-it-is", "What it is") }}"></div>
      {#- "Media", since the answers are audio and video: a select called "Audio"
          offering "With video" was a label contradicting its own list. -#}
      <label class="pick"><span>{{ t("library.page.media", "Media") }}</span><select class="well" id="audio"></select></label>
      <label class="pick"><span>{{ t("library.page.length", "Length") }}</span><select class="well" id="length"></select></label>
      {#- One name for one measure: the column below is "Level", and a select called
          "Difficulty" over it was two names for the same number. -#}
      <label class="pick"><span>{{ t("library.page.hard-words", "Level") }}</span><select class="well" id="difficulty"></select></label>
      <button type="button" class="clear" id="clear" hidden>{{ t("library.page.clear", "Clear") }}</button>
      </details>
      {#- How the list is ordered, for the shape that has no columns to press: a grid of
          cards with no way to reorder itself is a grid you can only scroll. It writes the
          same sort the table's headings do, and it is hidden under the table, where those
          headings say it already. -#}
      <div class="shape" id="sorts" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("library.page.order-them-by", "Order them by") }}"></div>
      {#- Cards or the table. The grid is how the page is browsed; the table is what it
          was, kept one press away because the complaint that retired the last card grid
          — that a card cannot be sorted — was a fair one. The choice is remembered. -#}
      <div class="shape" id="shape" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("library.page.how-to-show-them", "How to show them") }}"></div>
    </div>

    {#- The shelves, where the Library lands (design.md §12, "The Library is shelved by
        how much you'd follow", 2026-10-09): one row of texts for each band — Read it now,
        A stretch, Hard for now — and targum's own playlists, each band with a See all
        that is the list below under that band. Drawn by `library.js`. -#}
    <div class="shelves" id="shelves" hidden></div>
    {#- The Weekly portion (targum-internal#411): this Shabbat's first, then the year onward
        from it. Among the Library's shelves, because "which portion is it this week" is
        asked every week and the list cannot answer it. Every card opens the portion's reader, which is
        built for everybody, so nothing here builds or spends (#410). Drawn by
        `library.js` from `/portions`, and only on the Hebrew shelf. -#}
    <section class="portions" id="portions" aria-labelledby="portions-head" hidden>
      <div class="portions-top">
        <h2 class="portions-head" id="portions-head">{{ t("library.page.weekly-portion", "Weekly portion") }} <bdi class="portions-hebrew" lang="he" dir="rtl">פרשת השבוע</bdi></h2>
        <div class="seg" id="portion-schedule" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("library.page.which-calendar", "Which calendar") }}" hidden></div>
      </div>
      <ol class="portion-cards" id="portion-cards"></ol>
    </section>

    {#- Two shapes over one list: whichever is on is filled and the other is emptied, so
        nothing is ever drawn twice. The heading belongs to the table and is hidden with
        it — over a grid it is a row of sort buttons pointing at columns nobody can see. -#}
    <ul class="cards" id="cards"></ul>
    <div class="rows-head" id="rows-head" role="row"></div>
    <ul class="rows" id="catalogue"></ul>
    <p class="empty" id="picked-empty" hidden></p>
    <p class="tally" id="tally"></p>
  </section>


  {#- Somebody who has just failed to find what they wanted is exactly who needs the
      add page. A link, not a form: this page's job is browsing, and a pricing-and-
      progress flow living inside it would have made both jobs worse. -#}
  <p class="elsewhere">{{ t("library.page.not-here", "Not here?") }} <a href="/add">{{ t("library.page.upload-your-own", "Upload your own") }}</a>.</p>
</main>
{% include '_foot.html.j2' %}

<script>
window.TARGUM_KEY = "{{ token }}";
window.TARGUM_LANGUAGES = { {% for code, name in languages %}"{{ code }}": "{{ name }}"{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} };
window.TARGUM_CATALOGUE = {{ catalogue|tojson }};
window.TARGUM_COLLECTIONS = {{ collections|tojson }};
window.TARGUM_SETS = {{ sets|tojson }};
{% if strings %}window.TARGUM_STRINGS = {{ strings|tojson }};
{% endif %}</script>
<script>{{ asset('strings.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('charts.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('scenes.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('lang.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('sync.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('account.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('library.js') }}</script>
</body>
</html>
